# A member lookup through a qualifier that supplies nothing used to be compiled
# as the BARE NAME of the member, and the diagnostic then said
# `undefined variable (Foo)` — naming the attribute and never the qualifier
# that actually failed, so the message pointed at the wrong half of the line.
# It was the first wall on four html5lib files, where it read as a typo.
#
# `zlib` is a Pascal RTL unit wearing a Python module's name, and it is on the
# curated bare-import list in pasparser_proc.inc — which is what makes it
# resolve to the unit rather than to a host C header. THAT WAS A PREDICTION WHEN
# THIS LINE WAS WRITTEN and is a fact now: `zlib` joined PyRtlUnitServesPython in
# 80d71d782, nine hours AFTER this fixture landed (0997c6088). The fixture was
# never red for it, because it asserts an ERROR that both doors produce — which
# is exactly why the wrong comment survived.
#
# THIS FIXTURE USED `strings` AND THAT WAS WRONG FROM THE START. Its comment
# claimed `strings` was "a Pascal RTL unit wearing a Python module's name"; it
# is not reached that way at all. `strings` is DELIBERATELY absent from that
# list (an ordinary Pascal unit that happens to share a Python name), so the
# bare import falls through to /usr/include/strings.h, synthesises
# `libstrings.so` from the header's own file name, and produced a binary with a
# DT_NEEDED no loader can satisfy — verified against the PINNED compiler, whose
# build of `import strings` links and then dies at exec with
# `libstrings.so: cannot open shared object file`. The test never ran its
# binary, so it never saw that. Six of the seven RTL/header name collisions on
# this box (math, menu, netdb, png, regex, zlib) resolve to the unit; strings
# was the one that did not.
#
# THAT CENSUS IS WRONG FOR FOUR OF THE SIX, re-measured 2026-09-11 at 887b941bf.
# Only `math` and `zlib` are on the list; `menu`, `netdb`, `png` and `regex` take
# the HOST HEADER door exactly as `strings` did, and they are right to — all four
# have ZERO marshalling hits, i.e. they are ordinary Pascal units that merely
# share a C header's name, which is the distinction the curated list exists to
# record. The list is not a collision table.
#
# The instrument that said otherwise: a bare `import X` + `print(1)` references
# no symbol, so NO DT_NEEDED is synthesised under EITHER door and both doors read
# identically. The discriminator is code size against an import-free baseline —
# the header door costs +12288 bytes for every name (verified against an explicit
# `import 'zlib.h'`), while a real unit links its own content and no two differ
# by the same amount: math +90112, zlib +28672, and menu/netdb/png/regex all
# +12288, byte-identical, which four distinct units cannot be.
#
# The unloadable-binary half is no longer reachable and that is NOT this fixture's
# doing: symtab.inc now refuses at build time with `this build would die at exec`.
# Verified the same day, referencing a member of each: png, netdb and regex are
# REFUSED, and `menu` builds with a DT_NEEDED on libmenu.so.6, which is genuinely
# on the box. So the four are a naming question now, not a safety one.
# bug-n-an-attribute-on-an-unresolved-import-degrades-to-a-bare-name
#
# The companion test test_nilpy_bare_name_error_stays_short is the control: a
# genuinely bare undefined name must KEEP the short message, because there is
# no receiver to name and inventing one would be the same defect pointing the
# other way.

import zlib
print(zlib.Foo)
